\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} Proof: addFn is monotone.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} Strategy: case\PYGZhy{}split all 81 constructor combinations (3 × 3 × 3 × 3).}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} Most cases are trivial:}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{}   • unknown ≤ anything  →  addFn unknown ... = unknown ≤ anything.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{}   • anything ≤ conflict  →  addFn ... conflict = conflict, and anything ≤ conflict.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{}   • known m ≤ unknown is False  →  hypothesis contradiction closes the goal.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} The only non\PYGZhy{}trivial case is known/known on both sides, where the}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} hypotheses force the arguments (and hence both sides) to be equal.}
\PYG{c+c1}{\PYGZhy{}\PYGZhy{} simp\PYGZus{}all with the unfolded order closes every case.}
